### What is primary-replica architecture?

All writes go to one node (primary). Replicas receive a continuous stream of changes and serve reads.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class PrimaryReplicaConcept {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Primary-Replica architecture:");
        System.out.println("  Primary: accepts ALL writes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE)");
        System.out.println("  Replica 1: receives changes, serves reads");
        System.out.println("  Replica 2: receives changes, serves reads");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Write path: Client -> Primary -> commit -> replicate to replicas");
        System.out.println("Read path:  Client -> any Replica (or Primary)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Primary-Replica architecture:
  Primary: accepts ALL writes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE)
  Replica 1: receives changes, serves reads
  Replica 2: receives changes, serves reads

Write path: Client -> Primary -> commit -> replicate to replicas
Read path:  Client -> any Replica (or Primary)
```

### How does async vs sync replication work?

Async is fast but risks data loss. Sync is safe but adds latency. Semi-sync is the common middle ground.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class ReplicationModes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Async replication:");
        System.out.println("  Primary commits -> ACK to client immediately");
        System.out.println("  Ships changes to replicas afterward");
        System.out.println("  Fast, but if primary dies before shipping -> data lost");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Sync replication:");
        System.out.println("  Primary commits -> waits for replica ACK -> then ACK to client");
        System.out.println("  Safe, but every write is slower by network round-trip");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Semi-sync (common):");
        System.out.println("  Primary waits for ONE replica ACK -> ACK to client");
        System.out.println("  Balance: one replica guaranteed, minimal latency");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Async replication:
  Primary commits -> ACK to client immediately
  Ships changes to replicas afterward
  Fast, but if primary dies before shipping -> data lost

Sync replication:
  Primary commits -> waits for replica ACK -> then ACK to client
  Safe, but every write is slower by network round-trip

Semi-sync (common):
  Primary waits for ONE replica ACK -> ACK to client
  Balance: one replica guaranteed, minimal latency
```

### What happens when a replica dies?

Impact is limited: read traffic shifts to other replicas. No data loss. Writes unaffected.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class ReplicaFailure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Replica failure:");
        System.out.println("  Replica-2 crashes");
        System.out.println("  Read traffic shifts to Replica-1 and Primary");
        System.out.println("  No data loss (Primary still has everything)");
        System.out.println("  Writes completely unaffected");
        System.out.println("  When Replica-2 recovers: reconnects and catches up from log");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Replica failure:
  Replica-2 crashes
  Read traffic shifts to Replica-1 and Primary
  No data loss (Primary still has everything)
  Writes completely unaffected
  When Replica-2 recovers: reconnects and catches up from log
```

### What happens when the primary dies?

All writes stop. Recovery means promoting a replica. Risks: data loss and split-brain.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class PrimaryFailure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Primary failure:");
        System.out.println("  All writes STOP immediately");
        System.out.println("  Reads from replicas continue but grow stale");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Recovery: promote most up-to-date replica to new primary");
        System.out.println("  Manual: DBA runs promotion commands");
        System.out.println("  Automated: failover controller detects, selects, promotes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Risks:");
        System.out.println("  1. Data loss: writes primary committed but didn't replicate");
        System.out.println("  2. Split-brain: network partition -> two nodes both act as primary");
        System.out.println("     Fix: quorum + fencing (only one primary can exist)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Primary failure:
  All writes STOP immediately
  Reads from replicas continue but grow stale

Recovery: promote most up-to-date replica to new primary
  Manual: DBA runs promotion commands
  Automated: failover controller detects, selects, promotes

Risks:
  1. Data loss: writes primary committed but didn't replicate
  2. Split-brain: network partition -> two nodes both act as primary
```

### What about replication lag?

A client who writes and immediately reads from a replica may not see their own write.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ReplicationLag {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Replication lag scenario:");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh transfers 50000 (write to Primary)");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh views balance (read from Replica)");
        System.out.println("  Replica has not received the transfer yet");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh sees OLD balance -> confusing!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Mitigations:");
        System.out.println("  1. Read-your-own-writes: route to Primary briefly after write");
        System.out.println("  2. Track replication position: only read caught-up replicas");
        System.out.println("  3. Return write result directly: 'New balance: 950000'");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Replication lag scenario:
  Rajesh transfers 50000 (write to Primary)
  Rajesh views balance (read from Replica)
  Replica has not received the transfer yet
  Rajesh sees OLD balance -> confusing!
```

### What are the use cases?

Read scaling, high availability, backups, analytics offloading, and geographic locality.

| Use Case | How It Works |
|---|---|
| Read scaling | Offload heavy reads to replicas, free the primary for writes |
| High availability | Replicas hold full copies; failover keeps the system running |
| Backups | Take backups from a replica without slowing the primary |
| Analytics | Run expensive reports against a replica |
| Geo locality | Place replicas near users for lower read latency |

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class UseCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("At National Bank of India:");
        System.out.println("  Balance lookups (90% of traffic) -> Replica");
        System.out.println("  Transfers (10% of traffic) -> Primary");
        System.out.println("  Nightly reports -> Replica (no impact on Primary)");
        System.out.println("  Primary fails -> Replica promoted in 30 seconds");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
At National Bank of India:
  Balance lookups (90% of traffic) -> Replica
  Transfers (10% of traffic) -> Primary
  Nightly reports -> Replica (no impact on Primary)
  Primary fails -> Replica promoted in 30 seconds
```

### What is the limitation?

Primary-replica scales reads but not writes. All writes still go to one node. For write scalability, you need sharding.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class Limitation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Limitation: does NOT scale writes");
        System.out.println("  10 replicas -> 10x read capacity");
        System.out.println("  But still 1 primary -> same write capacity");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("For write scalability:");
        System.out.println("  -> Shard the database (split rows across multiple primaries)");
        System.out.println("  -> Each shard has its own primary-replica pair");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Limitation: does NOT scale writes
  10 replicas -> 10x read capacity
  But still 1 primary -> same write capacity

For write scalability:
  -> Shard the database (split rows across multiple primaries)
  -> Each shard has its own primary-replica pair
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Monitor replication lag as a first-class metric. Use semi-sync for critical data. Guard against split-brain with fencing and quorum.

```text
Senior checklist:
  [x] Monitor replication lag (Prometheus gauge per replica)
  [x] Semi-sync replication for critical banking data
  [x] Automated failover with quorum and fencing
  [x] Route read-your-own-writes to Primary
  [x] Old primary rejoins as replica after recovery
  [x] Understand: scales reads, NOT writes
  [x] For write scale: shard each shard with its own primary-replica pair
```
